  annotation = {中文辅助译名：用于脑电图情绪感知的稀疏贝叶斯神经网络的新型混合框架},
  abstract = {Electroencephalography (EEG)-based brain–computer interface systems are fundamentally challenged by the high noise levels and nonstationary characteristics of EEG signals, which often hinder robust feature extraction and reliable decision-making. Although Bayesian neural networks (BNNs) enable uncertainty modeling, conventional Gaussian priors impose uniform shrinkage and lack the flexibility required to suppress irrelevant EEG noise while preserving informative patterns. To address this limitation, this study proposes a hybrid sparse Bayesian neural network (HSBNN) that combines Gaussian scale mixture (GSM) and Horseshoe priors within a unified Bayesian framework. Specifically, GSM priors are employed to enhance representational flexibility during feature extraction, while the Horseshoe priors are used to enforce structured sparsity and enable automatic feature selection. Experiments conducted on the database for emotion analysis using physiological signals (DEAP) dataset demonstrate that HSBNN consistently outperforms state-of-the-art Bayesian and non-Bayesian methods. Moreover, comparable performance is maintained after pruning approximately 50% of the model parameters, indicating its potential for compact representation. Overall, these results suggest that HSBNN improves EEG emotion recognition performance while also providing a practical pathway toward sparse and interpretable BNNs for affective human–computer interaction systems.},
}

